# SHINOBU_23 Rationale - Quest DAG And Event Resolver

Date: 2026-05-18
Status: PENDING VERIFICATION

## Decision 09 - Spatial Hash Radius And Candidate Bound
Problem: Center-cell-only trigger insertion could miss large-radius triggers and made the 10,000-trigger claim dependent on a 27-cell neighbor scan.
Solution: Insert each trigger into every overlapped 100m cell, query only the player's current cell, and record `SpatialCandidateCount` in telemetry.
Rejected Alternatives: Full 27-cell query every tick or O(N) fallback scan. Both inflate candidate count on dense authoring and hide bad trigger placement.
Scalability potential: Low keeps bounded local-cell checks; Middle/High/Ultra can increase trigger density but still see candidate pressure in telemetry before gameplay truth bloats.
Hardware Impact: Low-end i3/MX350 avoids scanning adjacent empty cells and still catches radius overlap; expected gain is cell-density dependent, not profiler-proven.

## Decision 10 - Blackbox Dump Header And Chronology
Problem: Raw telemetry array dumps had no magic, entry size, cursor, or chronological ordering. Post-mortem could not distinguish stale slots from last frames.
Solution: `QuestDagTelemetryDump` writes a 32-byte header via MMF and copies the 300-entry ring oldest-to-newest from `TelemetryCursor`.
Rejected Alternatives: `File.WriteAllBytes(byte[])` raw blob or JSON. Raw blob is ambiguous; JSON allocates and is wrong for fatal path.
Scalability potential: Same 14.4KB telemetry payload across tiers; high/ultra get better diagnosis without gameplay struct bloat beyond 48B entries.
Hardware Impact: 0 us hot path. Fault path avoids managed byte array allocation and uses direct unsafe copy to MMF.

## Decision 11 - OSHINO Slot Preservation
Problem: Loader read `slot` but forced every node to `StateChunk = 0`, which would collapse multi-word OSHINO state when the compiler grows beyond the first 64-bit word.
Solution: Runtime chunk now derives from the two-bit OSHINO slot band (`slot >> 5`) and rejects out-of-vault chunks.
Rejected Alternatives: Keeping all binary nodes in chunk zero or interpreting authored strings at runtime. Both break data truth.
Scalability potential: Low first-hour data remains one word; Middle/High/Ultra can expand authored nodes into additional ulong chunks without changing resolver logic.
Hardware Impact: No hot cost beyond using the correct chunk index already present in runtime DTO.

## Decision 12 - No-Trigger Work Budget
Problem: No-trigger cascade nodes were scanned fully on every fixed-point pass, creating a possible `NoTriggerNodeCount * 5` tail outside the 10k spatial proof.
Solution: Added `NoTriggerNodesPerPassBudget = 256` and a Vault counter cursor. Telemetry sets `NoTriggerBudgeted` when work is spread across frames.
Rejected Alternatives: Recursive unlock chains or scanning every no-trigger node every tick. Both break predictability under bad authoring.
Scalability potential: Low can defer invisible narrative cascades; Middle/High/Ultra may raise the constant later with profiler proof.
Hardware Impact: Worst-case no-trigger checks are bounded per resolver pass, protecting low-end frame time at the cost of delayed non-spatial cascades.

## Decision 13 - Verification Without Killing Unity
Problem: Unity batchmode R5 exited before import because another Unity process holds the project lock.
Solution: Did not kill the active editor. Used source-backed Roslyn probe compilation with current Unity/ScriptAssemblies references to catch SHINOBU syntax/type errors.
Rejected Alternatives: Killing the developer/agent Unity process or claiming Unity proof from a lock-aborted log.
Scalability potential: Compile hygiene only; keeps parallel agent work intact.
Hardware Impact: No runtime impact.
